 it for  yourself  bring me back  [Music]  oh wait I almost forgot to mention our  huge giveaway of one of our four M2  MacBook Air models that we ordered and  to join all you have to do is subscribe  to this Channel and comment one time on  each one of our launch week MacBook Air  videos including this one which will  give you more entries to win before we  announce the winner at random right here  on YouTube on July 25th and even better  we'll ship it to you no matter where you  are in the world for free including the  shipping charges and import taxes now  getting into the second problem we of  course have the thermal throttling  issues and it's not only when doing  extreme workloads it's when doing fairly  common stuff as well like exporting 50  photos in Lightroom and what we're  seeing is that the M2 MacBook Air is  heating up much faster than the M1 model  and that's because the new Air doesn't  even have a proper heatsink according to  iFixit with their new territory down it  simply uses this thin metal shielding  which doesn't really do much at all for  cooling and that's really disappointing  because the previous M1 MacBook Air came  with an actual heatsink but this new  model that uses more power with the M2  Chip doesn't an apple could have  actually used this leftover empty space  on the side of the logic board as you  can see in this area from our teardown  to put in an actual heatsink and then  connect that to the M2 Chip using some  sort of heat pipe if they moved some of  the things around on the logic board but  they didn't they have no proper heatsink  but thankfully there is a solution and  that is our thermal pad mod which we did  for only 15 bucks so all you got to do  is buy one of these sheets cut it up and  place it over the logic board like we  showed you guys in our thermal  throttling fix video and just doing this  alone made the M2 MacBook Air finish the  photo export faster than the M2 MacBook  Pro which is fan powered it is a massive  difference and the only drawback is at  the bottom of the MacBook Air is going  to be getting hot during extended  workloads but the benefit is that you  get 15 minutes of full performance  without reaching massive throttling  instead of just five minutes without the  thermal pads and you get the new M2  MacBook Air redesign without having to  go for that old M2 MacBook Pro Design  just to get that fan to avoid this easy  throttling and now getting into problem  number three this also has to do a  little bit with thermal throttling  because the M2 MacBook Air at the base  price comes with an 8 core GPU but if  you really want to you could upgrade to  the 10 core version for a hundred  dollars more and it seems like you  should be getting a lot more GPU  performance however according to a video  from Greg's gadgets there was actually  some cases where the 10 chords you model  performed worse than the a core which  makes no sense at all but what ends up  happening is that the 10 core heats up  faster and thermal throttles so much  that it actually down clocks the GPU  leading to slightly worse performance  than the 8 core model which should not  happen especially if you're paying a  hundred dollars more and it shows that  this thermal system is pretty bad but  luckily you can use that thermal pad mod  to get a little bit longer full  performance without throttling and now  getting into problem number four we have  this new midnight finish which I  absolutely love it almost looks like a  matte black when it's dark and when it's  bright it looks like a dark navy blue  and I just love this color however it  does have some issues because underneath  this finish we have a regular silver  metal color so what ends up happening is  if you scratch [ __ ] it's gonna be  incredibly noticeable and not only that  we noticed some little silver lines or  scratches near the Thunderbolt Port area  after only using it a couple of times so  I can imagine after a year or two this  thing could look all scratched up  especially near the port area and not  only that but this thing is a  fingerprint magnet like crazy you just  use it a couple times fingerprints  everywhere you're gonna have to clean it  all the time so that could be annoying  and that could be a problem to some of  you guys so definitely choose a  different color if you hate fingerprints  and those scratches moving on to problem  number five another issue with the M2  MacBook Air is that once again it still  only supports One external monitor now  that is very disappointing because all  of us were expecting Apple to upgrade  this and allow up to two monitors but no  still only one so there are a lot of  disappointed people out there especially  typically people who like to use  multiple monitor setups and not only  that but we still have the same  Bluetooth 5.0 and Wi-Fi 6 specs which is  disappointing there's so many other  laptops and machines and products out  there that have moved up to Bluetooth  5.2 and I personally was expecting us to  get the new specs but no it's all the  same now for problem number six we have  the microphones which we thought would  be getting better especially with this  full redesign on this M2 MacBook Air but  no we compared them to the M1 MacBook  Air and it actually seems like the M1  previous model sounds better in terms of  the microphone quality which we did not  expect and this kind of sounds even more  quiet and a little bit muted so that's  disappointing in terms of the  microphones now that's definitely not  going to be a Make It or Break It  decision for you guys but keep that in  mind if you do a lot of Zoom calls or  whatever else now for problem number  seven we have the new keyboard and  unfortunately this one has a little bit  less travel than the previous M1 model  it seems like because this thing got  thinner they did have to reduce the key  travel and some people might care a lot  maybe not but that is one thing that is  good to know and on top of that the  trackpad also seems different as well it  doesn't seem as clicky as it was before  I don't know if that's because it got  thinner or they changed something but I  definitely prefer the clickiness and  feel of the trackpad and keyboard on the  previous M1 model now of course this is  not going to be a huge deal but once  again it's something to keep in mind and  now getting to some of the really big  problems we have problem number eight  which is the SSD speeds on the base 256  gig model just like the M2 MacBook Pro  we have the same issue where we only  have a single nand chip instead of using  up both of those spots like apple did in  the past with the M1 MacBook Air and pro  and this is important because both of  those spots have channels going to the  SSD controller and when you take out one  of those channels by not putting a chip  there it essentially slows down the SSD  speeds and while it seems like  performance is not going to get impacted  that much we did a real world file  transfer and the 512 gig SSD model  finished over six times faster than the  256 and that is just so disappointing  not only that but those SSD channels  matter a lot for a virtual swap memory  performance so when you use up all of  your RAM it starts to rely on the SSD to  act as virtual RAM and because the speed  is slower it ends up slowing down the  entire machine when you're doing some  multitasking and not even extreme  multitasking just like five Safari tabs  while doing a productivity task can slow  down the performance drastically across  a a bunch of different tests so I  believe Apple really messed up with this  SSD situation and our recommendation is  to avoid the base SSD as much as  possible just go for that 512 gig and up  and then problem number nine is actually  connected to that because this new Mt  MacBook Air starts at twelve hundred  dollars which is two hundred dollars  more than the previous M1 MacBook Air  but you have this SSD issue where Apple  took away one of the nand chips so  you're paying 200 more for half of the  nand chips which definitely sucks as a  consumer with this base model and to  properly fix this issue you've got to  upgrade it to the 512 gig SSD to revive  the actual virtual memory swap  performance that you should be getting  or that the M2 Chip actually deserves  and that's going to cost you an  additional two hundred dollars so as  essentially you have a 400 difference  for the same virtual memory swap  performance going from the thousand  dollar M1 MacBook Air to the 1400 M2  MacBook Air with a 512 gig SSD so that's  a little bit disappointing and that's a  big difference in cost for the same  virtual memory swap performance and for  file transfers as well and not only that  but you're paying 200 extra compared to  the M1 and you're no longer getting a  proper heatsink you're just getting that  thin shielding which really doesn't do  much at all so the cost difference is  really a problem on this M2 MacBook Air  and now finally for problem number 10  Apple showed off the new Max safe 3  feature with fast charging that'll get  you from zero percent to fifty percent  in just 30 minutes which sounds amazing  and apple did a great job marketing it  however if you go out and you buy the  base model you go to your Apple Store  Your Best Buy which usually just stocks  the base model you're not gonna get fast  charging support yes if you want fast  charging you have to upgrade to the 67  watt power adapter which costs an  additional twenty dollars to get or you  have to upgrade to a higher end model to  get that included which sucks because  the base model only gets the 30 watt  adapter which does not feature fast  charging so definitely keep that in mind  if you're excited about that feature and  there you guys go those were the top 10  problems with the M2 MacBook Air that  you need to be aware of before you make  your purchase don't forget to click  subscribe to enter the giveaway as well  as commenting down below be sure to  check out one of those two videos right  there and if you're interested in one of  those grid Studio frames as a gift I  have links below to that as well thanks  for watching and we'll see you in the  next video  foreign

